<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><div class="page-hero__grid"><div class="page-hero__copy"><p class="breadcrumb"><a href="<?php echo esc_url( home_url( '/' ) ); ?>">Home</a> / Results</p><p class="eyebrow">Results</p><h1>What LexCollect did inside a working firm.</h1><p class="lead">LexCollect was built in the finance department of a high-volume firm with a $21M receivables book spread across MyCase, Filevine, LawPay, HubSpot, QuickBooks and the bank. Everything below happened there. Every number came out of the system, not a slide deck.</p></div></div></div></section><section class="hm-proof" aria-label="Headline results"><div class="container"><ul class="hm-proof__grid"><li data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="0"><span class="hm-proof__num" data-count="21.4" data-decimals="1" data-prefix="$" data-suffix="M">$21.4M</span><span class="hm-proof__label">receivables reconciled to the firm&#x27;s ground truth, within 5%, the gap explained and assigned</span></li><li data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="80"><span class="hm-proof__num" data-count="3.5" data-decimals="1" data-prefix="$" data-suffix="M">$3.5M</span><span class="hm-proof__label">in contract balances re-linked to the right clients through a migration between two CRMs</span></li><li data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="160"><span class="hm-proof__num" data-count="1395" data-decimals="0" data-prefix="" data-suffix="">1,395</span><span class="hm-proof__label">records reconciled in that migration, with 0 mismatches needing manual correction</span></li><li data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="240"><span class="hm-proof__num" data-count="228" data-decimals="0" data-prefix="" data-suffix="">228</span><span class="hm-proof__label">willing payers with a failed card ($928K) reached with one message instead of a collections call</span></li></ul></div></section><div class="subnav-scope"><nav class="subnav" aria-label="Result stories" data-spy="true"><div class="container"><ol><li><a href="#reconciliation" class="is-active"><span>01</span>Reconciliation</a></li><li><a href="#payments"><span>02</span>Payment matching</a></li><li><a href="#migration"><span>03</span>Migration</a></li><li><a href="#hard-debt"><span>04</span>Hard debt</a></li><li><a href="#trust"><span>05</span>Trust &amp; accounts</a></li><li><a href="#escalations"><span>06</span>Escalations</a></li><li><a href="#growth"><span>07</span>Growth</a></li></ol></div></nav><section class="section rs-stories"><div class="container"><article class="rs-story" id="reconciliation"><header class="rs-story__head" data-reveal="true"><p class="pf-module__eyebrow"><span>01</span>Reconciliation</p><h2>A $21M book, reconciled to the firm&#x27;s own ground truth.</h2><p class="rs-story__lede">The firm&#x27;s “true AR” lived in a spreadsheet built from raw exports. The database said something else. LexCollect closed the gap and explained what was left.</p></header><div class="rs-story__body"><aside class="rs-ledger" aria-label="Reconciliation: key figures" data-reveal="true"><p class="rs-ledger__head">Key figures</p><dl><div><dt>$21,425,589</dt><dd>the firm&#x27;s ground-truth receivables figure</dd></div><div><dt>&lt; 5%</dt><dd>gap after reconciliation, attributable to a named unmatched-payment backlog and timing</dd></div><div><dt>428</dt><dd>contracts carried as “Paid” with only ~4% of their value collected, reclassified</dd></div><div><dt>561</dt><dd>orphan contracts worth $3.5M with no client, re-linked or given a client record</dd></div><div><dt>89</dt><dd>duplicate client records merged, references moved, nothing hard-deleted</dd></div></dl></aside><div class="rs-notes"><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Zombie “Paid” contracts.</strong> A prior import had marked 428 contracts as paid simply because they weren&#x27;t in the firm&#x27;s AR spreadsheet. Only about 4% of their value had ever been collected. LexCollect found them by comparing status to actual payments, reclassified them as abandoned, and kept every change reversible.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Orphan contracts.</strong> 561 contracts had balances but no client attached. 27 were re-linked through reference numbers embedded in the contract, 3 by normalized name, 2 were flagged as ambiguous for a person, and 529 got a proper client record created from the contract itself. Every merge and link was written to an audit table.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Duplicates.</strong> Exact and near-duplicate client records were merged into one survivor, with all 23 related tables repointed, so a client&#x27;s messages, payments, plan and escalations finally sat in one place.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Self-verifying.</strong> Each cleanup migration checked itself against the ground truth before committing, so a fix that moved the book by more than $50K would have stopped itself.</p></div></div></article><article class="rs-story" id="payments"><header class="rs-story__head" data-reveal="true"><p class="pf-module__eyebrow"><span>02</span>Payment matching</p><h2>Thousands of processor payments, tied to the right client.</h2><p class="rs-story__lede">The processor knew who paid. The client system knew who owed. Nothing connected the two, especially when a spouse, parent or employer was the one paying.</p></header><div class="rs-story__body"><aside class="rs-ledger" aria-label="Payment matching: key figures" data-reveal="true"><p class="rs-ledger__head">Key figures</p><dl><div><dt>2,013</dt><dd>payments ($1,190,204) matched to a client, contract and invoice</dd></div><div><dt>759</dt><dd>payments ($370,447) queued for human confirmation with ranked suggestions</dd></div><div><dt>243</dt><dd>new unmatched payments arrived in six days, which is why matching runs continuously</dd></div></dl></aside><div class="rs-notes"><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Suggestions, not guesses.</strong> For every unmatched payment, LexCollect ranks the five most likely clients using name similarity, amount against the client&#x27;s installment, and timing against their due date. Nothing is auto-matched below a confidence threshold. A person confirms, and the confirmation is recorded with who did it and how.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Real-world names.</strong> ALL-CAPS processor names, corrected names left in a notes field, two surnames in a different order. The matcher reads all of them.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Booked once.</strong> Payment webhooks retry and handlers race. Idempotency guards mean a payment can&#x27;t be booked twice no matter how many times the processor sends it.</p></div></div></article><article class="rs-story" id="migration"><header class="rs-story__head" data-reveal="true"><p class="pf-module__eyebrow"><span>03</span>Migration</p><h2>A mass financial migration between two incompatible CRMs, without losing a dollar.</h2><p class="rs-story__lede">Moving every client&#x27;s balance, plan and history from MyCase to Filevine is where receivables usually get lost. LexCollect reconciled both systems against the firm&#x27;s own client lists before, during and after the move.</p></header><div class="rs-story__body"><aside class="rs-ledger" aria-label="Migration: key figures" data-reveal="true"><p class="rs-ledger__head">Key figures</p><dl><div><dt>$3.5M</dt><dd>in contract balances re-linked to the right clients during the move</dd></div><div><dt>1,395</dt><dd>records in the new system reconciled against 548 records on the firm&#x27;s own lists</dd></div><div><dt>448 / 196 / 18</dt><dd>exact, reference-number-only and name-only matches, each tier reviewed separately</dd></div><div><dt>0</dt><dd>mismatches needing manual correction</dd></div><div><dt>733</dt><dd>records in the new system that weren&#x27;t on any firm list, surfaced for intake to confirm</dd></div></dl></aside><div class="rs-notes"><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Direct payment sync.</strong> Filevine payments now post straight into LexCollect through a webhook, with no middleware. Each one matches by invoice number, updates the invoice and contract balances, and logs an activity on the account, so the next message reflects it.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>History, through the same door.</strong> Historical Filevine payments were backfilled through the identical booking pipeline as live ones, so old and new money follow one set of rules.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>The old system kept in the picture.</strong> Contacts, engagements, ledgers and payment plans from the previous system are synced into the client 360, so staff still see the full history during and after the transition.</p></div></div></article><article class="rs-story" id="hard-debt"><header class="rs-story__head" data-reveal="true"><p class="pf-module__eyebrow"><span>04</span>Hard debt</p><h2>Delinquent dollars, reconnected by message and measured by bucket.</h2><p class="rs-story__lede">Once the book was reconciled, the firm could see its delinquent balance, split it by age, and reach every account with the right message instead of another round of calls.</p></header><div class="rs-story__body"><aside class="rs-ledger" aria-label="Hard debt: key figures" data-reveal="true"><p class="rs-ledger__head">Key figures</p><dl><div><dt>$15.7M</dt><dd>delinquent and late receivables made visible by aging bucket and owner</dd></div><div><dt>$928K</dt><dd>across 228 willing payers with a failed card, separated from true delinquency</dd></div><div><dt>86%</dt><dd>of the “autopay decline” turned out to be clients finishing their plans, not a card leak</dd></div></dl></aside><div class="rs-notes"><p data-reveal="true"><strong>The right list.</strong> Consult-only contacts, raw import noise and abandoned contracts are classified and kept out of the queue by default, so every message and every conversation lands on a real account.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>A record of every contact.</strong> Outcome, dollars, origin and commission are logged per activity, whether it was an email, an SMS or a conversation. The firm&#x27;s old activity dashboard had been showing about 8% of real activity. LexCollect now certifies per-person figures from the full log.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Promises that get followed.</strong> Payment commitments carry a date and an amount, and missed installments surface while they&#x27;re still small.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Willing payers treated differently.</strong> The failed-card segment gets an “update your card” email instead of a collections call, which protects the relationship and recovers money faster.</p></div></div></article><article class="rs-story" id="trust"><header class="rs-story__head" data-reveal="true"><p class="pf-module__eyebrow"><span>05</span>Trust &amp; firm accounts</p><h2>Operating, trust and the books, reconciled as one.</h2><p class="rs-story__lede">Retainers and advance fees have to land in trust. Earned fees have to land in operating. The firm has to be able to prove both.</p></header><div class="rs-story__body"><aside class="rs-ledger" aria-label="Trust &amp; accounts: key figures" data-reveal="true"><p class="rs-ledger__head">Key figures</p><dl><div><dt>Trust / IOLTA</dt><dd>routing for retainer and advance-fee deposits, separate from operating</dd></div><div><dt>Every txn</dt><dd>processor transactions reconciled to a client, contract and invoice, or queued</dd></div><div><dt>Monthly</dt><dd>certified AR snapshots, so history is real history</dd></div><div><dt>5 cohorts</dt><dd>HubSpot “won” deals validated against payment evidence before a client counts as active</dd></div></dl></aside><div class="rs-notes"><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Trust and operating, kept apart.</strong> Payments taken in LexCollect are routed to the firm&#x27;s operating account or client trust account (including IOLTA) based on what they&#x27;re for, so a retainer never lands in the wrong place.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>The CRM and the books agree.</strong> A deal marked won in HubSpot only becomes an active client when there&#x27;s payment evidence behind it. Deals with no money yet are held for review rather than inflating the client count.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Locked down.</strong> Row-level security on every table, no anonymous reads of financial data, and an audit log of who changed what.</p></div></div></article><article class="rs-story" id="escalations"><header class="rs-story__head" data-reveal="true"><p class="pf-module__eyebrow"><span>06</span>Unique escalations &amp; internal connection</p><h2>Internal hand-offs with a queue, a priority and an owner.</h2><p class="rs-story__lede">Before, an escalation was an email. Now it&#x27;s a record that billing, service teams and management all see, with a status that has to be closed.</p></header><div class="rs-story__body"><aside class="rs-ledger" aria-label="Escalations: key figures" data-reveal="true"><p class="rs-ledger__head">Key figures</p><dl><div><dt>7</dt><dd>hand-off queues: service teams, compliance, customer care, management, sales, billing ops, plus a custom one</dd></div><div><dt>9</dt><dd>source contexts, from client reply to service-team request to refund follow-up</dd></div><div><dt>4</dt><dd>priorities, with urgent and high surfaced to the right inbox automatically</dd></div></dl></aside><div class="rs-notes"><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Inboxes per department.</strong> Service teams see what needs someone who knows the client. Management sees what needs a decision. Billing sees what&#x27;s theirs. The same escalation, three views.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Hardship handled, not hidden.</strong> Hardship requests are their own workflow, so a client in trouble gets a considered answer instead of another message.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Deadlines respected.</strong> A deadline watch shows the firm&#x27;s commitments on the client record and firm-wide, so collections never collides with a promise the firm has made.</p></div></div></article><article class="rs-story" id="growth"><header class="rs-story__head" data-reveal="true"><p class="pf-module__eyebrow"><span>07</span>Growth you can trace</p><h2>More paying clients, and revenue with a source on it.</h2><p class="rs-story__lede">Every collected dollar in LexCollect carries its origin, message and outcome, so the revenue the system produced is a report, not an estimate.</p></header><div class="rs-story__body"><aside class="rs-ledger" aria-label="Growth: key figures" data-reveal="true"><p class="rs-ledger__head">Key figures</p><dl><div><dt>7</dt><dd>origin buckets on every collected dollar: AR list, email sequence, SMS, follow-up, transfer, service-team request, and more</dd></div></dl></aside><div class="rs-notes"><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Segments, not blasts.</strong> Email segments come from reconciled balances: failed card, missed installment, paid in full, no invoice yet. The right client gets the right message, and the response shows up as a payment with that segment as its origin.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Paying clients, counted honestly.</strong> A client counts as paying when there&#x27;s a payment behind them, not when a deal is marked won. The monthly view shows current and on-plan clients gained and lost, so growth is net, not gross.</p><p data-reveal="true"><strong>Revenue attribution.</strong> Dollars from the Connect queue, from a commitment kept, from an email or SMS sequence and from a transfer are summed separately, by person and by month. That&#x27;s the number you put next to the LexCollect invoice.</p></div></div></article></div></section></div><section class="section section--deep section--rule rs-onboard"><div class="container"><div class="section-head" data-reveal="true"><p class="eyebrow">Suited to your firm</p><h2>Everything above is configuration at your firm, not a rebuild.</h2><p class="lead">Your team comes from a live roster. Escalation targets are your departments. Message sequences, product lines, aging buckets, outcome labels and queues are set per firm. <a href="<?php echo esc_url( lexcollect_page_url( 'contact' ) ); ?>">Tell us about your firm.</a></p></div><ol class="tl"><li class="tl__item" data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="0"><span class="tl__kicker">Week 1</span><h3>Connect and reconcile</h3><p>We connect your systems, pull history and show you the first reconciled book with the gaps named.</p></li><li class="tl__item" data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="90"><span class="tl__kicker">Week 2</span><h3>Configure the firm</h3><p>Roles, roster, queues, product lines and escalation targets set to match how you already work.</p></li><li class="tl__item" data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="180"><span class="tl__kicker">Week 3</span><h3>Work the queue</h3><p>Sequences go out to reconciled lists. Your team works the clients who need a person, with the ledger in front of them.</p></li><li class="tl__item" data-reveal="true" data-reveal-delay="270"><span class="tl__kicker">Month 1</span><h3>First certified snapshot</h3><p>A receivables number the partners can trust, with the trend starting from a real baseline.</p></li></ol></div></section><section class="cta-band"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Book a call</p><h2>Want these numbers for your firm?</h2><p class="lead">One conversation, your systems, and a first look at what&#x27;s late, what&#x27;s unmatched and what&#x27;s recoverable now.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn btn--primary" href="<?php echo esc_url( lexcollect_booking_url() ); ?>">Book a call <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><a class="btn btn--ghost" href="<?php echo esc_url( lexcollect_page_url( 'platform' ) ); ?>">Explore the platform</a></div></div></section>
